WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.504
Authentication monitoring turns identity events into operational visibility.

2
00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:25.049
Useful signals include successful sign-ins, failed login patterns, account lockouts, new device events, suspicious MFA activity, privileged account sign-ins, service account activity, password reset events, MFA reset events, token anomalies, and changes to authentication policy.

3
00:00:25.049 --> 00:00:29.755
The goal is to detect meaningful risk without drowning teams in noise.

4
00:00:29.755 --> 00:00:31.829
Some signals require context.

5
00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:43.237
Impossible travel concepts at a high level may indicate suspicious access, but travel, network routing, remote work, and privacy tools can complicate interpretation.

6
00:00:43.237 --> 00:00:49.858
A new device event may be expected after a laptop replacement or suspicious after a password reset.

7
00:00:49.858 --> 00:00:57.197
Failed logins may represent user error, credential stuffing at a high level, or a service misconfiguration.

8
00:00:57.197 --> 00:00:58.792
Triage matters.

9
00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.542
Monitoring should connect to escalation and response.

10
00:01:02.542 --> 00:01:08.844
Alerts need owners, severity expectations, investigation steps, and containment options.

11
00:01:08.844 --> 00:01:23.203
Response might include disabling an account, revoking sessions, resetting credentials, reviewing MFA enrollment, rotating service credentials, contacting an application owner, or preserving audit trails.

12
00:01:23.203 --> 00:01:30.622
Authentication data is often central to incident response because identity is the path to many other systems.

13
00:01:30.622 --> 00:01:34.610
Privacy-aware logging is part of responsible operations.

14
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:47.534
Logs should provide enough detail to investigate and audit authentication activity without unnecessarily exposing passwords, tokens, regulated data, or sensitive personal information.

15
00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:57.904
Strong programs define what is logged, who can access it, how long it is retained, and how it supports compliance, detection, support, and forensic needs.
